  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 149 PM CDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nUpper-level ridging will shift eastward and become less amplified \nMonday into Tuesday. This will will allow a short wave trough to \nmove out of Mexico and southwestern Texas and into the Middle to \nLower Missouri valley by Tuesday evening. Forcing associated with \nthis shortwave may be enough to wring out the marginal moisture \navailable to the system. As a result, isolated to scattered showers, \nand potentially a thunderstorm or two, look possible during the day \nacross eastern Kansas and western Missouri. The potential for \nmeasurable rainfall is about 20-30%. The potential for any storms is \nstill very low and generally less than 10%. So, while there is \nsome potential, it's more than likely just going to be showers \nmoving through, if there is any precipitation. \n\nOtherwise, above normal temperatures will continue. For the Kansas \nCity area, normal high temperatures range from 63 on Monday, to 60 \nby Sunday. Forecast highs on Monday are in the upper 70s, about 15 \ndegrees above normal for this time of year. Temperatures drop off \nslightly on Tuesday with the shortwave and associated cloud cover \nand precipitation chances moving into the area through the day.\nFor the middle part of the work week, while the ridging remains\nmostly situated southeast of the region, persistent southerly \nflow around the ridging/ surface high to our southeast, will \nmaintain warm advection across the Southern and Central Plains. \nSo it looks likely we'll see highs rebound back into the upper \n70s to potentially lower 80s, about 15-20 degrees above normal. \nLate in the week, precipitation chances start to increase, with \nscattered to widespread rainfall by Saturday. Thursday, a front \nsags southward into the area, pushed southward by a shortwave \ntracking across the Northern Plains and Upper Midwest. That \nfront interacts with increasing moisture from the persistent \nsoutherly flow to give us our increasing precipitation chances. \nFor the weekend, a series of waves moves through the area, with \nfront still in the vicinity. With broad upper forcing, the \nboundary in the area, and moisture available, we should see \nscattered to widespread showers Friday night into Saturday. \nRight now, it looks like a 40-60% chance for rain from Saturday\nafternoon into Saturday evening, with decreasing chances after \nmidnight. So umbrellas may be needed for anyone outside during \nthat time frame. Still, though it looks like we'll see scattered\nto widespread precipitation, rain amounts look relatively low \nthrough the weekend. For the 48 hour period ending at 6 am \nSunday morning, there is roughly a 1 in 3 (30-35%) chance for \nrain amounts exceeding 1 inch and just over a coin flip (50-60%)\nfor exceeding half an inch.",
